{"results":[{"url":"https://guides.loc.gov/this-month-in-business-history/july/bretton-woods-conference","final_url":"https://guides.loc.gov/this-month-in-business-history/july/bretton-woods-conference","title":"Research Guides: This Month in Business History: Bretton Woods Conference &amp; the Birth of the IMF and World Bank","description":"Compiled by specialists in Business Reference Services at the Library of Congress, this guide highlights stories about the people, places, and events that made their mark on business history.","language":"en","text":"Skip to Main Content\n\n\n# This Month in Business History\n\n# Bretton Woods Conference & the Birth of the IMF and World Bank\n\nAcme Newspictures, Inc. photograph. *Participants of the United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference meet at the Mount Washington Hotel in Bretton Woods, N.H..* 1944. New York World-Telegram and the Sun Newspaper Photograph Collection. Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division.\n\nThe Bretton Woods Conference, officially known as the United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference, was a meeting of delegates from 44 nations that met from July 1 to 22, 1944 in Bretton Woods, New Hampshire. The purpose of the conference was to agree upon a series of new rules for the monetary system after World War II.\n\nThe genesis of the 1944 conference dates back to the Atlantic Conference in 1941, but by early 1942, more detailed plans were being developed with the ideas from Harry Dexter White, a Special Assistant to the U.S. Secretary of the Treasury, and John Maynard Keynes, an advisor to the British Treasury, being the two major competing schools of thought. In mid-June 1944, a preliminary conference was held in Atlantic City and on July 1, 1944, the Bretton Woods Conference convened.1\n\nThe conference resulted in the creation of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the International Bank for Reconstruction & Development (IBRD) though that quickly became the World Bank. It was intended that the IMF was to maintain a system of fixed exchange rates centered on the U.S. dollar and gold, while the IBRD was responsible for providing financial assistance for the reconstruction of war-ravaged nations and the economic development of less developed countries.2 The IMF and IBRD formally came into existence on December 27, 1945. The World Bank opened it doors on June 25, 19463 and made its first loan in 1947.4\n\nWhile the Bretton Woods system was in place until President Richard Nixon ended the dollar’s convertibility to gold in 1971, both the IMF and World Bank are still operating today.\n\nToday, the IMF is an organization of 190 member countries with approximately 2,700 staff from 150 countries. The World Bank includes 189 member countries with staff from more than 170 countries and offices in over 130 locations, and is a family of five organizations including the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD) International Development Association (IDA), International Finance Corporation (IFC), Multilateral Investment Guarantee Agency (MIGA), and International Centre for Settlement of Investment Disputes (ICSID).\n\n## Featured Recording\n\nChristine Lagarde, managing director of the International Monetary Fund (IMF), delivered the Kissinger Lecture in Foreign Policy and International Relations at the Kluge Center on December 4, 2018. Lagarde spoke about the changing landscape of the international system and the need for continued creativity in U.S. leadership in order to tackle the world's shared economic challenges. Afterwards, Lagarde was interviewed by Margaret Brennan of *Face the Nation* on CBS.\n\n## Print Resources\n\nThe following titles link to fuller bibliographic information in the Library of Congress Online Catalog. Links to digital content are provided when available.\n\n* The Battle of Bretton Woods : John Maynard Keynes, Harry Dexter White, and the Making of a New World Order\n\n  by\n\n  Benn Steil\n\n  Call Number: HG255 .S837 2013\n\n  ISBN: 9780691149097\n\n  Published/Created: 2013-02-24\n\n  A history of the creation of the postwar economic order created in July 1944 with two of the central figures being of John Maynard Keynes, the renowned and revolutionary British economist, and Harry Dexter White, a self-made American technocrat.\n* Bretton Woods : Birth of a Monetary System\n\n  by\n\n  Armand Van Dormael\n\n  Call Number: HG205 1944 .D67 1978b\n\n  ISBN: 0333233697\n\n  Published/Created: 1978-06-28\n\n  This is a general history of the conference and history of the international monetary systems born in the aftermath of World War II.\n* Bretton Woods Agreements : A Bibliography\n\n  by\n\n  Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\n\n  Call Number: Z7164.F5 U455\n\n  Published/Created: 1946\n\n  This is a bibliography covering the years 1943-1945.\n* The Bretton Woods Agreements : Together with Scholarly Commentaries and Essential Historical Documents\n\n  by\n\n  Naomi Lamoreaux (Editor); Ian Shapiro (Editor)\n\n  Call Number: HG3881.5.W57 B734 2019\n\n  ISBN: 9780300236798\n\n  Published/Created: 2019-07-23\n\n  Commentaries by top scholars alongside the most important documents and speeches concerning the Bretton Woods Conference of 1944. The two world wars brought an end to a long-standing system of international commerce based on the gold standard. After the First World War, the weaknesses in the gold standard contributed to hyperinflation, the Great Depression, the rise of fascism, and ultimately World War II. The Bretton Woods Conference of 1944 arose out of the Allies' desire to design a postwar international economic system that would provide a basis for prosperity, trade, and worldwide economic development.   Alongside important documents and speeches concerning the adoption and evolution of the Bretton Woods system, this volume includes lively, readable, original essays on such topics as why the gold standard was doomed, how Bretton Woods encouraged the adoption of Keynesian economics, how the agreements influenced late twentieth century ideas of international development, and why the agreements ultimately had to give way to other arrangements. Available in the De Gruyter Online database.\n* The Bretton Woods Transcripts\n\n  by\n\n  Kurt Schuler (Editor); Andrew Rosenberg (Editor); Jacques de Larosière (Preface by); Steve H. Hanke (Preface by)\n\n  Call Number: HG205 .U54 1944\n\n  ISBN: 9781941801017\n\n  Published/Created: 2013-01-01\n\n  This is a verbatim record of meetings of the conference that established the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank. The Bretton Woods conference was held in July 1944 and included delegates from 44 countries attended the conference. The conference transcripts were never intended for publication, and give record of what participants at a major international gathering said behind closed doors.\n* Doc[uments] no. [1]-547\n\n  by\n\n  United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference, Bretton Woods, N.H., 1944\n\n  Call Number: HG205 1944.A3\n\n  Published/Created: 1944\n\n  This is material from the conference that established the Bretton Woods Agreement.\n* The First Half Century : An Overview\n\n  by\n\n  K. Sarwar Lateef\n\n  Call Number: HG3881.5.W57 L28 1994\n\n  ISBN: 0821330780\n\n  Published/Created: 1994-01-01\n\n  This is 50 anniversary look at the history of the World Bank.\n* The IMF in a Changing World, 1945-85\n\n  by\n\n  Margaret Garritsen de Vries; International Monetary Fund Staff\n\n  Call Number: HG3881.5.I58 D39 1986\n\n  ISBN: 0939934655\n\n  Published/Created: 1986-06-15\n\n  This collection of 18 articles, originally published in Finance & Development and the IMF Survey, was written by Margaret Garritsen de Vries and constitutes a short history of the IMF's evolution.\n* International Monetary Fund. Articles of agreement between the United States of America and other powers, formulated at the United nations monetary and financial conference, Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, July 1 to July 22, 1944, signed at Washington Decem\n\n  by\n\n  United States. Department of State\n\n  Call Number: KZ235.32 .U55 no. 1501\n\n  Published/Created: 1945\n\n  See 1501 at Hathi Trust External for a digitized copy.\n* Just Before Bretton Woods : The Atlantic City Conference, June 1944\n\n  by\n\n  Kurt Schuler (Editor); Gabrielle Canning (Editor)\n\n  Call Number: HG3881 .J87 2019\n\n  ISBN: 9781941801055\n\n  Published/Created: 2019-10-15\n\n  The Bretton Woods, New Hampshire conference that established the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank in 1944 was highly publicized then and remains well known today. The secretive Atlantic City, New Jersey conference that occurred immediately before Bretton Woods and laid the groundwork for it has never received much attention. The conference was notable for the presence of John Maynard Keynes, the most famous economist of the 20th century, as leader of the British delegation, and Harry Dexter White, later revealed to have passed secrets to the Soviet Union, as leader of the American delegation. Kurt Schuler and Gabrielle Canning have collected the conference minutes and related documents, and have added an introduction, annotations, and commentary to make them readily understandable. *Description from publisher*\n* Proceedings : Annual Meeting of the Board of Governors\n\n  by\n\n  International Bank for Reconstruction and Development\n\n  Call Number: HG3881.5.W57 P763\n\n  Published/Created: 2nd (Sept. 11-17, 1947)-4th (Sept. 13-16, 1949)\n* Summary Proceedings ... Annual Meeting of the Board of Governors\n\n  by\n\n  International Monetary Fund\n\n  Call Number: HG3881 .I61a\n\n  Published/Created: 1947-\n\n## Library of Congress Digital Resources\n\nThe following resources created and digitized by the Library of Congress can be used to find out more about the Bretton-Woods and the creation of the IMF and World Bank as well as other events of the day.\n\n* Chronicling America, Historic American Newspapers\n\n  Historic American newspapers selected and contributed by state partners in the National Digital Newspaper Program. Set the dates beginning in 1944 and search on words and phrases like like Bretton Woods, John Maynard Keynes, Harry Dexter White, United Nations Monetary, etc.\n* United States Statutes at Large\n\n  This was the law (55 Stat. 512) to \"provide for the participation of the United States in the International Monetary fund and the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development.\" It was Public Law 171.\n* Digital Image Collections\n\n  There are a number of images of the Mount Washington Hotel, as well as a few of John Maynard Keynes and Harry Dexter White.\n\n## Internet Resources\n\nThese online resources provide more information on the topic and include resources you can search for images, news articles and more.\n\n* This is a history of the World Bank but chapter one looks specifically at Bretton Woods.\n* On August 15, 1971, Nixon addressed the nation on a new economic policy and took the US off of the gold standard from Federal Reserve History. He also issued Executive Order 11615 External and Proclamation 4074. External\n* This a text of the agreements that were adopted at the United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference (Bretton Woods, New Hampshire) on July 22, 1944. They were originally accepted by 29 countries and since then have been signed and ratified by a total of 190 Member countries. There are also links to related documents.\n* This is a digitized set of hearings held June 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 25, and 28, 1945.\n* The Federal Reserve's FRASER project has digitized many documents related to the Bretton Woods Conference including the Proceedings and Documents of the United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference, Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, July 1-22, 1944.\n* This is a UK Archives archived page that contains digitized material related to the International Monetary Fund and World Bank, the Bretton Woods conference, the Bretton Woods System in practice, and the Demise of Bretton Woods.\n* Contains many digitized documents related to conference proceedings including Transcripts that were never intended for publication.\n* This is the entry for Bretton Woods that is part of the \"10 Best and 10 Worst U.S. Foreign Policy Decisions\" from the Council on Foreign Relations that provides more of the political context including the different perspectives of Lord John Maynard Keynes and Treasury official Harry Dexter White.\n* A general history of the Bretton Woods conference from Federal Reserve History.\n* The subscription resources marked with a padlock  are available to researchers on-site at the Library of Congress. If you are unable to visit the Library, you may be able to access these resources through your local public or academic library.  \n  A search for articles in the following business and historical newspaper databases will produce relevant results.   \n  Some recommended databases are:  \n  - Accessible Archives  \n  - America's Historical Newspapers, 1690-1922  \n  - America: History & Life  \n  - Annals of American History  \n  - Early American Imprints, Series I Evans 1639-1800  \n  - ProQuest Historical Newspapers\n* The International Monetary Fund (IMF) was created in 1945 and is now an organization of 190 countries, working to foster global monetary cooperation, secure financial stability, facilitate international trade, promote high employment and sustainable economic growth, and reduce poverty around the world. There is an  Archive. External\n* This was originally published in 1951 and includes books, pamphlets, and periodical articles published since the Fund was organized in 1946, include publications describing the organization and functions of the International Monetary Fund and also comments and analyses of its activities.\n* The NBER has a many digitized documents related to economics and does include material related to the Bretton Woods conference including \"The Bretton Woods International Monetary System: A Historical Overview\" (1991/1993) and \"An Essay on the Revived Bretton Woods System\" (2003).\n* Founded in 1944, the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development—soon called the World Bank—has expanded to a closely associated group of five development institutions. The focus shifted to development, with a heavy emphasis on infrastructure such as dams, electrical grids, irrigation systems, and roads. There is an  Archive. External\n\n## Search the Library's Catalog\n\nAdditional works on this topic in the Library of Congress may be identified by searching the Library of Congress Online Catalog under appropriate Library of Congress subject headings. Choose the topics you wish to search from the following list of subject headings to link directly to the Catalog and automatically execute a search for the subject selected. Please be aware that during periods of heavy use you may encounter delays in accessing the catalog. For assistance in locating other subject headings that may relate to this subject, please consult a reference librarian.\n\n## Notes\n\n* Last Updated: Nov 4, 2026 9:45 AM\n* URL: https://guides.loc.gov/this-month-in-business-history\n* Print Page\n\nLogin to LibApps\n\nSubjects: \n\n* American History\n* Business and Management\n\nHosted by Springshare","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":731.3739479996002,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}